# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 3: Weather Description
#
# The WHEN task for the funfair photo of sub-lessons 1–2: the weather, and a
# guess at the time of year and time of day. The examiner asks it on almost
# every picture, and it is the one place in this theme where the candidate is
# allowed to *infer* rather than report — so the point of this file is the
# hedging vocabulary (`ech mengen`, `warscheinlech`, `et gesäit aus wéi`). The
# learner must be able to say "it is probably summer" without claiming it as fact.
#
# The photo detail this describes: blue sky with a few scattered clouds, bright
# sun casting short shadows at the bottom of the frame, no rain, people in
# T-shirts and shorts — a warm summer afternoon.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION, same as 01 and 02 — no @question prompts, no first-person
# opinion. A hedged inference *about the photo* ("it is probably summer") is not
# an opinion: it is an observation with a confidence marker, and a stranger can
# still check it against the picture. `Ech ginn gär op d'Fouer` could not be
# checked, which is why nothing of that shape is here.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as the rest of the theme: present tense, simple main
# clauses, no attributive adjectives on masculine or neuter nouns (which is why
# there is no `en waarme Nomëtteg` here — `Et ass en Nomëtteg am Summer`
# instead).
#
# Words sub-lessons 1–2 already teach (d'Bild, uewen, ënnen, gesinn, d'Leit,
# blo, …) are deliberately NOT repeated — the pass-gate makes them
# prerequisites and stat keys are shared across the app.
#
# The three @fill blocks carry this theme's highest-value frames, because the
# hedge is a *pattern* that transfers to every picture and every topic: the
# `Ech mengen, …` opener, the `well`-clause that justifies a guess (verb-final —
# read, not assembled), and the two-clause weather report. Each has a comma
# before the blank, which is also why the Eifeler Regel cannot bite in them.
# See references/content-contract.md.

@lesson P1.03 "Schueberfouer: Weather Description"

# Same photo as 01, declared again: @image is per-file, and the learner must see
# the photo during THIS sub-lesson too. The alt text differs on purpose — it
# describes the detail this file drills.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/schueberfouer/img/schueberfouer.webp"
@image-alt "The same funfair photo, seen for the weather: a bright blue sky with a few white clouds, strong sunshine casting short shadows, everyone in summer clothes."

# --- Weather ---
@word d'Wieder = the weather
@word den Himmel = the sky
@word d'Sonn = the sun
@word d'Wollek = the cloud
@word d'Wolleken = the clouds
@word de Reen = the rain
@word de Schiet = the shadow
@word schéngen = to shine
@word sonneg = sunny
@word waarm = warm
@word dréchen = dry
@word schéin = nice

# --- Time of year and time of day ---
@word de Summer = the summer
@word d'Joreszäit = the season
@word de Nomëtteg = the afternoon
@word d'Woch = the week
@word mëttes = at midday

# --- Hedging a guess ---
@word mengen = to think
@word warscheinlech = probably
@word villäicht = maybe
@word well = because

@sentence
@lu D'Wieder ass schéin a sonneg.
@en The weather is nice and sunny.
@distractor-lu Wollek
@distractor-lu Reen
@distractor-en cloud
@distractor-en rain

@sentence
@lu D'Sonn schéngt an den Himmel ass blo.
@en The sun is shining and the sky is blue.
@distractor-lu Wollek
@distractor-lu gréng
@distractor-en cloud
@distractor-en green

@sentence
@lu Um Himmel sinn e puer Wolleken.
@en In the sky there are a few clouds.
@distractor-lu Sonn
@distractor-lu Beem
@distractor-en sun
@distractor-en trees

@sentence
@lu Op dem Bild ass kee Reen.
@en In the picture there is no rain.
@distractor-lu Schiet
@distractor-lu Wollek
@distractor-en shadow
@distractor-en cloud

@sentence
@lu D'Wieder ass waarm an dréchen.
@en The weather is warm and dry.
@distractor-lu kal
@distractor-lu naass
@distractor-en cold
@distractor-en wet

@sentence
@lu Ech gesinn e Schiet ënnen um Bild.
@en I see a shadow at the bottom of the picture.
@distractor-lu Wollek
@distractor-lu uewen
@distractor-en cloud
@distractor-en top

@sentence
@lu Et gesäit aus wéi de Summer.
@en It looks like summer.
@distractor-lu Wanter
@distractor-lu Fréijoer
@distractor-en winter
@distractor-en spring

@sentence
@lu D'Joreszäit ass warscheinlech de Summer.
@en The season is probably summer.
@distractor-lu Wieder
@distractor-lu Wanter
@distractor-en weather
@distractor-en winter

@sentence
@lu Et ass villäicht en Nomëtteg am Summer.
@en It is maybe an afternoon in summer.
@distractor-lu Wanter
@distractor-lu Wollek
@distractor-en winter
@distractor-en cloud

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The hedge opener — the single most transferable frame in the whole exam track:
# `Ech mengen, …` prefixes any guess, on any picture or topic. The comma both
# marks the clause boundary and exempts the blank from the Eifeler Regel. Season
# nouns are the blank; both distractors are seasons contradicted by the photo.
@fill
@lu Ech mengen, et ass [Summer].
@en I think it is [summer].
@distractor-lu Wanter
@distractor-lu Hierscht
@distractor-en winter
@distractor-en autumn

# The justification frame. `well` sends the verb to the end (`schéngt` last), so
# the learner reads that order rather than assembling it — the @fill/@sentence
# split again. Blank is the hedging adverb.
#
# Distractor choice is load-bearing here and the throwaway builder audit caught
# it: `sécher` ("certainly") was the obvious-looking distractor and is WRONG as
# one — it is grammatical *and* coherent in the slot, so it is a second correct
# answer, breaking R3. The distractors are nouns instead, which cannot occupy an
# adverb slot before a bare noun, plus `net`, which contradicts the `well`-clause
# offered as the reason.
@fill
@lu Et ass [warscheinlech] Summer, well d'Sonn schéngt.
@en It is [probably] summer, because the sun is shining.
@distractor-lu net
@distractor-lu Wanter
@distractor-lu Wollek
@distractor-en not
@distractor-en winter
@distractor-en cloud

# The two-clause weather report — the shape a candidate should reach for when
# asked about the weather anywhere. Two blanks in different clauses and
# different word classes (adjective, then noun), so they cannot swap.
@fill
@lu D'Wieder ass [sonneg], an ech gesinn e puer [Wolleken].
@en The weather is [sunny], and I see a few [clouds].
@distractor-lu naass
@distractor-lu Reen
@distractor-en wet
@distractor-en rain

# The time-of-day guess. Two blanks of different word classes (time adverb, then
# noun after the fixed preposition `der`) so they cannot swap, and `wärend der`
# stays in the frame — that is what makes the second blank unambiguous. The
# distractors are the other times of day, each wrong for a sunlit photo with
# short shadows.
@fill
@lu Ech mengen, et ass [mëttes] wärend der [Woch].
@en I think it is [midday] during the [week].
@distractor-lu moies
@distractor-lu nuets
@distractor-en in the morning
@distractor-en at night
